  "reason": "The records collectively establish several relevant principles: cooling rate can kinetically retain metastable phases (especially R10–R11), solidification or composition can produce Laves-containing and compositionally segregated multiphase HEAs (R1, R13, R14, R16–R17, R19–R20), and an antiferromagnetic constituent can reduce saturation magnetization in a ferromagnetic HEA (R5). They also show that interfaces and hard secondary phases can provide pinning or phase-coupling effects. However, none describes rapid quenching of FeCoNiTi to retain the specific fcc+C14 constitution, the comparison with a compositionally matched C14-dominant alloy, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, or C14-assisted magnetic domain-wall pinning and enhanced coercivity. Thus the literature points broadly toward phase control and magnetic dilution but leaves the specific intervention-mechanism combination as a substantial new idea."
